number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|Delegated password reset reaches a cloud administrator|An unapproved principal has effective reset-password authority over an AD user AND the verified synchronized Entra identity has an active privileged role AND AD remains its password authority.|Object-specific effective reset right; SID and anchor mapping; synchronization scope and mode; active role assignment; authentication context.|Use separate cloud-native administrative identities and restrict source-account reset delegation.|A reset right is not MFA bypass. Cloud-managed passwords, a disabled account, or no applicable password flow breaks this specific path.|T1098;T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6;IA-5|Limit who can replace an administrator's authenticator and enforce least privilege on the source account.|MS-CA|High
002|Source-account ACL control reaches a cloud administrator|An unapproved principal can change the DACL or owner of a source AD user AND the resulting effective access can grant account-control rights AND that identity maps to an active cloud administrator.|Security descriptor; owner; inherited and explicit ACEs; effective-access derivation; authoritative identity link; scoped cloud role.|Remove the unintended ACL-control path and separate the cloud administrative account.|Do not equate GenericWrite with password reset. A protected object with an effective deny or an unsupported property write is not a demonstrated control path.|T1098;T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6;CM-5|Restrict changes to authorization data and the privileges that protect the administrative identity.||High
003|One identity administers both AD and the tenant|The same verified identity has effective AD control-plane membership AND an active privileged Entra role across the synchronization boundary.|Resolved privileged-group membership; AD identity; exact cloud mapping; active role scope; organizational tier policy.|Separate directory administration from cloud administration and remove unneeded standing privileges.|Similar names do not establish one identity. Eligible-only cloud roles need their activation requirements evaluated separately.|T1078.002;T1078.004|AC-5;AC-6|Separate administrative duties and prevent one account's compromise from carrying both privilege sets.|MS-PRIVILEGE|High
004|An AD-controlled identity can activate a privileged cloud role|A source identity is controllable outside its approved tier AND its mapped Entra user is eligible for a privileged role AND documented activation requirements do not add an independent approval or authentication boundary.|Source control path; PIM eligibility and role scope; activation policy; applicable Conditional Access and authentication requirements.|Protect the source identity and require independent controls for privileged role activation.|Eligibility alone is not active privilege. Unknown approval, authentication context, or activation state makes the role-reachability conclusion Unknown.|T1548.005;T1078.004|AC-2;AC-6;IA-2|Govern temporary privileges and verify identification requirements at activation.|MS-PRIVILEGE;MS-CA|High
005|A roastable service identity also holds cloud privilege|An enabled AD account with an SPN uses a password-based service identity AND the verified synchronized account has an active privileged cloud role.|SPNs; account type; supported encryption; effective password policy and rotation metadata; cloud role assignment.|Remove human cloud roles from service identities and use an appropriately managed workload identity.|An SPN does not prove a password can be cracked. AES support or a gMSA changes the practical exposure and must be reported.|T1558.003;T1078.004|AC-6;IA-5|Reduce unnecessary service-account privilege and protect service authenticators.|MS-GMSA|High
006|A no-preauthentication account carries cloud privilege|Kerberos preauthentication is disabled on an enabled AD account AND its verified synchronized Entra account has an active privileged role.|Account-control flags; account type; source mapping; role assignments; effective authentication and password policy.|Restore preauthentication where supported and separate the privileged cloud identity.|Do not request AS-REP material. Configuration establishes exposure, not successful password recovery.|T1558.004;T1078.004|IA-2;IA-5;AC-6|Require authentication safeguards and avoid amplifying a weak authenticator with cloud privilege.|MDI-ACCOUNTS|High
007|Offboarding has not removed cloud authorization|An authoritative offboarding record or disabled AD account is older than the approved propagation window AND the mapped Entra account remains enabled with a sensitive active grant.|Time-stamped lifecycle event; current AD and Entra state; synchronization health; sensitive grants; approved propagation window.|Complete identity disablement and entitlement removal in both directories and investigate the failed lifecycle step.|A disabled AD account does not prove an active cloud session. Guest and cloud-managed identities need their own lifecycle authority.|T1078.004|AC-2;AC-6|Reconcile account lifecycle and remove access that no longer has an approved business purpose.|MS-TOKENS|High
008|A delegated source-account path coincides with weak cloud authentication coverage|IDENTITY.001 or IDENTITY.002 has evidence for a specific user AND a failed relevant SCuBA MFA or legacy-authentication control is confirmed applicable to that user's actual authentication scenario.|Underlying path evidence; original SCuBA control ID/version/status; user and resource scope; enabled policies; exclusions; supported authentication methods.|Break the source-account control path and correct the applicable cloud authentication gap.|A tenant-wide SCuBA failure alone is not a per-user MFA bypass. A matching policy in report-only mode is not enforcement.|T1098;T1078.004|AC-6;IA-2;IA-5|Protect both the source authenticator and the independent cloud authentication boundary.|MS-CA|High
009|A source-anchor edit can take authority over an eligible cloud identity|An unapproved principal can make the required effective source-anchor change AND a unique valuable cloud target is eligible for that hard match AND current tenant and cloud-side hard-match protections permit the specific operation.|Source write ACL; exact configured anchor; unique target and immutable ID; source-authority state; target role/eligibility; hard-match protections; supported flow.|Protect anchor-writing authority and enable supported takeover protection without disrupting approved provisioning.|From July 2026, protected existing or privileged targets are blocked in documented cases. Writeback exceptions and ongoing sync are distinct. Never assert a generic privileged-account takeover.|T1098;T1556.007|IA-4;AC-3;CM-5|Protect identifier binding and changes in identity authority.||High
010|A permitted soft match can bind an unintended source to a cloud identity|An unapproved source editor can satisfy a supported unique soft-match condition for a valuable cloud target AND current target eligibility and soft-match protections allow the authority change.|Specific UPN or primary SMTP matching inputs; uniqueness; target eligibility; BlockSoftMatchEnabled; verified supported provisioning behavior.|Protect authoritative matching attributes and restrict unneeded soft matching using supported controls.|UPN equality is not proof of a match. Hard-match protections cannot be assumed to govern soft match, and privileged/protected target constraints still apply.|T1098;T1556.007|IA-4;AC-3;CM-5|Preserve intended identity binding at the provisioning boundary.||High
